#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""rumination-watch — the entity's "voice in the head": re-touch open threads, speak only on change.

WHY THIS EXISTS (djbclark, 2026-10-24)
--------------------------------------
An agent session is silent between prompts: nothing re-touches what it left open, so a stall, an
unread message or a forgotten job is only noticed when a human asks "why did you stop?". A human
mind does the opposite — it *ruminates*: it periodically re-touches open loops without being asked.
This is that loop, with the two constraints djbclark set on it:

1. **Continuous but nearly free.** It is a launchd script with NO model in it. It reads local files
   and compares them to the last run's state; it costs nothing until there is something to say.
2. **Not disruptive to thought in progress.** It stays silent unless something *changed* or is
   *due*, and it speaks in one line. A session mid-task is not interrupted by a status echo.

It is deliberately the same shape as `load-watch` and `fleet-watch` (launchd, no model, relays a
brief), because that pattern is already proven here and already understood by the operator.

WHAT IT WATCHES (each an "open loop")
-------------------------------------
- **Mail**: unread messages in the sibling mailbox (`agents/comm/mailboxes/<role>/new/`).
- **Jobs**: unfinished bigteam job records (`~/.local/state/bigteam/*/jobs/*.json` with no `closed`).
- **Stuck jobs**: a job whose `done` path exists but whose record is still open.
- **Open PRs** authored by the operator's account (via `gh`; best-effort, offline is not an incident).
- **Chain staleness**: the Tier 1 log's `head_sha` vs the repo's real HEAD.
- **Next move**: the seat is `idle` (relay.py `--why`) and the Tier 1 log declares next steps. See below.

AND IT DEPLOYS ITSELF (issue #143)
----------------------------------
The launchd job executes this file FROM the shared djbclark-ade checkout, so a merge into that repo
reaches the job only when the checkout moves — it did not, and the two fixes for "the voice is never
heard" sat merged and unrun for hours while `last pass` looked healthy. Each pass therefore
fast-forwards its own checkout to `origin/<branch>` (fast-forward only, clean tracked tree only, never
while another git operation is in progress) and logs `deploy updated <old> -> <new>`; a refusal is
logged once per cooldown with its reason. `RW_NO_DEPLOY=1` turns it off.

IT SPEAKS WHEN THE SEAT IS IDLE WITH DECLARED WORK (2026-10-30)
----------------------------------------------------------------
Everything above fires on a CHANGE, so an idle seat with a full `- Next steps:` list was told
nothing for as long as nothing else moved: 216 consecutive quiet passes, 18.1 hours (2026-10-26 00:30
to 18:37) with a healthy watcher and a seat nobody had started. `seat_dark("idle")` is "" on purpose
(an idle seat is healthy), so `escalate_dark` cannot see it either. So one keyed, state-like item,
`next move: N declared step(s) still open ...`, goes to the SEAT when ALL hold: the seat's `--why`
probe (one per pass, cached by `seat_word`; the send paths add one more after an unproven send) says
`idle` (never `working`, `silent`, `card:*`, `unreachable` or unreadable: those are busy, or already
covered by the darkness page), the Tier 1 log (the same file `chain_staleness` reads, via
`resolve_pointer`) has N >= 1 OPEN steps under `- Next steps:`, and the last nudge settled more than
RW_IDLE_NUDGE_S ago. A bullet that says there is nothing to do (`- None`, `NOTHING IS PENDING ...`,
`- No further work`) is not a step and is not counted: otherwise the natural way for a seat to say "I
am done" would keep nudging it every cooldown forever, with no ack that could stop it. The line
carries the COUNT and never a step's text: several chains write to one pointer, and naming a step
would send this seat to do another seat's slice. It goes to the seat, not djbclark: it is not his
decision. The one exception is the tool's standing rule for ANY line — if its send comes back
`unverified`, the disclosure that an admitted loss happened still goes to him. Knobs:
`RW_IDLE_NUDGE=0` turns it off; `RW_IDLE_NUDGE_S` is the cooldown (default 10800, 3 h), stamped only
by a send that settled.

TWO OUTPUTS, TWO AUDIENCES (djbclark, 2026-10-24)
-------------------------------------------------
- **To the session** (a one-line relay): "here is work you left open" — actionable by the agent.
- **To djbclark, via the `dann` quiet topic** (`inbox-target dann`), **only** when something needs
  HIS decision. Rate-limited by a per-item cooldown, because the point of a quiet channel is quiet.

OPT-IN ABSENT-SEAT START (djbclark's ruling, 2026-10-30, option 3)
---------------------------------------------------------------
RW_AUTO_SEAT=1 may start a fresh seat ONLY on a proven `gone` diagnosis, narrowing the "including
after a hard error" ruling. Off by default until field-tested; RW_AUTO_SEAT_PROVIDER must be set
explicitly (no model or thinking flags). If relay cannot diagnose a UUID-shaped id, one extra
`paseo agent inspect --json -- <id>` probe (up to 20 s, no model) must confirm AGENT_NOT_FOUND;
errors, timeouts, unreadable bodies and unknown shapes never prove absence. See the decision table
in `auto_seat`. A `refused`/`unknown` seat does not auto-start and still pages the human when no owner
resolves. A `silent` hung seat is NOT replaced, so the individual can stay dark until the human acts.
RW_AUTO_SEAT_S (default 3600 s): at most one attempt per role per window, confirmed or not. A failed
start with no id becomes eligible again only after the window; returned ids are retained for diagnosis.
Every invocation outcome is logged; only pre-invocation refusals are suppressed once per window.
The prompt names the absolute Tier 1 file, never step text. The darkness page still goes out and
names any automatically started id. The start runs as `env -u PASEO_AGENT_ID` (a caller's
PASEO_AGENT_ID makes the new seat that agent's SUBAGENT; `bin/self-slash` clears it for the same
reason) and, once `paseo agent inspect` confirms the seat, opens its tab with `paseo agent open`:
a `paseo run` agent has NO tab, and an invisible seat hitting a `card:*` (which §J never answers)
is the same darkness one level down (djbclark's ruling, 2026-10-30). A failed open is logged,
carried into the page to djbclark and never fails the start; a seat that comes alive only by
recovery (its start's confirmation was lost, or its id came from the Paseo listing) gets ONE
attempt per id on the first pass that diagnoses it live, and pages him if that fails; a page
no send accepted is CARRIED (`undisclosed_tab`) and retried, never dropped for good;
RW_AUTO_SEAT_NO_OPEN (1/true/yes/on) skips the attempt on a host with no desktop.

Exit codes: 0 nothing to say (the normal case); 10 something to say — and it was PRINTED, whether or
not the seat took it (a refused pass exits 10 too, with the line carried to the next pass); 2
usage/error. The exit code is the interface for launchd and for a session that runs it by hand.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import fcntl
import json
import math
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Iterator

HOME = Path(os.environ.get("HOME", "/Users/djbclark"))
STATE_DIR = Path(os.environ.get("RW_STATE_DIR", HOME / ".local/state/rumination-watch"))
STATE = STATE_DIR / "state.json"
LOG = STATE_DIR / "rumination.log"

REPO = Path(os.environ.get("RW_REPO", HOME / "src/techno-euphora"))
ROLE = os.environ.get("RW_ROLE", "dann")
MAILBOX = REPO / "agents" / "comm" / "mailboxes" / ROLE / "new"
BIGTEAM = HOME / ".local/state/bigteam"

# Job records are owned by the session that opened them; only OUR OWN are actionable here. Matched
# as a prefix so both a live session id and the role label work ("cline-techno-euphora-…",
# "dann-8d891459"). Empty means "no ownership filter" (report everything recent) — useful when
# debugging the watcher itself, wrong for the launchd job.
OWNER = os.environ.get("RW_OWNER", ROLE)

def _env_int(name: str, default: int, minimum: int = 1) -> int:
    """An int knob, CLAMPED. `RW_PENDING_MAX=0` used to keep everything (`lines[-0:]` is the whole
    list) and `RW_PR_LIMIT=0` made the cap marker fire on an empty listing ("at least 0 open PR(s)"):
    both fail in the dishonest direction. A non-number falls back to the default instead of dying at
    import time, which is how a mistyped env var takes the watcher down for good.

    Defined here, above every knob, because the knobs are exactly the lines that used to call `int()`
    unguarded: `RW_COOLDOWN_S=6h` or `RW_DEPLOY_TIMEOUT_S=1m` raised at import and killed the WHOLE
    watcher — pass, `status` and voice — so one typo in one environment variable was a permanent,
    silent outage of the thing that exists to prevent outages.
    """
    raw = os.environ.get(name, "")
    if not raw:
        return default
    try:
        return max(minimum, int(raw))
    except ValueError:
        return default


# How often the same item may be re-announced to djbclark (seconds). A quiet channel that repeats
# itself is not quiet; this is the anti-noise budget djbclark asked for.
DJB_COOLDOWN_S = _env_int("RW_COOLDOWN_S", 6 * 3600)

# The checkout THIS FILE runs from, which is not REPO: launchd executes this script as a tracked file
# in the djbclark-ade shared checkout, while REPO is techno-euphora (whose chain log it reads). A
# merge into djbclark-ade does not reach a launchd job until someone pulls, and nobody did: on
# 2026-10-29 the checkout sat five commits behind master and the job ran pre-#131 code for two days,
# logging `session=False` 870 times while the entity heard nothing (issue #143). So the pass brings
# ITS OWN checkout forward, and says so in the log when it cannot.
SELF_REPO = Path(os.environ.get("RW_SELF_REPO") or Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1])
DEPLOY_OFF = os.environ.get("RW_NO_DEPLOY", "") not in ("", "0", "false", "no")
# A refusal that repeats every 300 s is noise; the word is logged once and again after this long.
DEPLOY_COOLDOWN_S = _env_int("RW_DEPLOY_COOLDOWN_S", 6 * 3600)
DEPLOY_TIMEOUT_S = _env_int("RW_DEPLOY_TIMEOUT_S", 60)
# What makes a fast-forward unsafe. A tree with only UNTRACKED files is safe (git will not touch
# them); a merge, rebase, cherry-pick or bisect in progress is not, and neither is a detached HEAD.
GIT_BUSY = ("MERGE_HEAD", "CHERRY_PICK_HEAD", "REVERT_HEAD", "BISECT_LOG", "rebase-merge",
            "rebase-apply", "sequencer")


def log(msg: str) -> None:
    STATE_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    with open(LOG, "a") as fh:
        fh.write(f"{time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z')} {msg}\n")


def normalise_pending(raw: list) -> tuple[list[dict], int]:
    """Queued lines as `{"key": str|None, "text": str, "queued_at": float|None}`, plus how many entries
    could not be read. Accepts the old bare string (no key, no age) and the mapping; anything else is
    counted, not guessed at: the old `str(x)` turned a stray `5` into a line sent to the seat."""
    out: list[dict] = []
    bad = 0
    for entry in raw:
        if isinstance(entry, str) and entry:
            out.append({"key": None, "text": entry, "queued_at": None})
        elif isinstance(entry, dict) and isinstance(entry.get("text"), str) and entry["text"]:
            key = entry.get("key")
            queued = entry.get("queued_at")
            # A finite number or nothing: a nan/inf/bool age would crash `_age` or print nonsense.
            stamp = float(queued) if _is_num(queued) and not isinstance(queued, bool) else None
            if stamp is not None and not math.isfinite(stamp):
                stamp = None
            out.append({"key": key if isinstance(key, str) and key else None,
                        "text": entry["text"], "queued_at": stamp})
        else:
            bad += 1
    return out, bad


def _age(seconds: float) -> str:
    """`45s`, `6m`, `3h`: how long a queued line has waited, for the log."""
    seconds = max(0, int(seconds)) if math.isfinite(seconds) else 0
    if seconds < 90:
        return f"{seconds}s"
    if seconds < 90 * 60:
        return f"{round(seconds / 60)}m"
    return f"{round(seconds / 3600)}h"


def load_state() -> dict:
    """The last pass's state. Anything hand-edited into the wrong SHAPE is discarded, not crashed on."""
    try:
        data = json.loads(STATE.read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        return {}
    # A list where a mapping belongs used to raise AttributeError mid-pass (the whole watcher down,
    # silently, for as long as the file stayed wrong); a string `pending` was iterated character by
    # character. Neither is reachable except by hand — which is exactly when a watcher must not die.
    for key in ("last", "told", "stuck", "nudged", "seated"):
        if not isinstance(data.get(key), dict):
            data[key] = {}
    # `pending` entries are `{"key", "text", "queued_at"}` since 2026-10-30 (see `_pass`); before that
    # they were bare strings, and a state file written by the previous version must keep its queue.
    # Normalise HERE so the pass sees one shape. An entry that is neither (a number, a mapping with
    # no text) is dropped from the in-memory queue and COUNTED in `pending_unreadable`, which `_pass`
    # logs and pops; the file itself is only rewritten by a pass that saves state, as always.
    raw_pending = data.get("pending", [])
    if not isinstance(raw_pending, list):
        raw_pending = []
    data["pending"], unreadable = normalise_pending(raw_pending)
    if unreadable:
        data["pending_unreadable"] = unreadable
    # Shapes are not values. A hand edit that leaves `told` a string timestamp or a `stuck` count a
    # word used to kill the pass at `float(last)` / `int(...)` — the same hand-edit threat the shape
    # guards above are for, one level down. Drop only the unreadable entries; keep the rest.
    data["told"] = {k: v for k, v in data["told"].items() if _is_num(v)}
    data["stuck"] = {k: v for k, v in data["stuck"].items() if _is_num(v)}
    data["nudged"] = {k: v for k, v in data["nudged"].items() if _is_num(v)}
    data["seated"] = {k: v for k, v in data["seated"].items() if _is_num(v)}
    return data


def _is_num(value: object) -> bool:
    """A number we can do arithmetic with. JSON can hand us a string, a list or null here.

    Non-finite counts as unreadable (`"1e999"` parses to `inf`): a stamp of `inf` would silence the
    item it guards FOREVER, and no later pass can heal it, because the pass that never fires never
    re-stamps. Dropping it is the only self-healing answer.
    """
    try:
        return math.isfinite(float(value))          # type: ignore[arg-type]
    except (TypeError, ValueError, OverflowError):
        return False


def save_state(data: dict) -> None:
    """Atomic replace: a half-written state file would make the next run re-announce everything."""
    STATE_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = STATE.with_suffix(".json.tmp")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(data, indent=1, sort_keys=True))
    os.replace(tmp, STATE)


def run_split(cmd: list[str], timeout: int = 20) -> tuple[int, str, str]:
    """(rc, stdout, stderr) with the two streams kept APART. Anything parsing JSON must use this.

    `fleet.py` prints a warning on stderr on its SUCCESSFUL `list --json` path (`messaging_warning`,
    fleet.py:1593), and `gh` warns the same way. Gluing stderr onto the body turns a healthy read
    into an unparseable one — and, because an unparseable body was read as "no rows", into a fleet
    that looks empty and a page telling him to start a seat that is already running.
    """
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)
        return p.returncode, p.stdout.strip(), p.stderr.strip()
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return 127, "", ""


def run(cmd: list[str], timeout: int = 20) -> tuple[int, str]:
    """Both streams for logging callers. argv list, never a shell string: no quoting problems here."""
    rc, out, err = run_split(cmd, timeout)
    return rc, (out + ("\n" if out and err else "") + err).strip()


# --- deploying itself ----------------------------------------------------------------------------


def _git(args: list[str], timeout: int = 20) -> tuple[int, str, str]:
    """git against SELF_REPO, with `-C` so nothing depends on the process's working directory."""
    return run_split(["git", "-C", str(SELF_REPO), *args], timeout=timeout)


def running_sha() -> str:
    """The commit this job is EXECUTING, which is the only sha that says what is live."""
    rc, out, _ = _git(["rev-parse", "--short=10", "HEAD"])
    return out if rc == 0 and out else "unknown"


def _deploy_say(state: dict, word: str, msg: str, now: float) -> None:
    """Log a deploy outcome, but say a repeated refusal ONCE per cooldown.

    Every pass is 300 s apart, so an unfixable "skipped: dirty" would otherwise write the same line
    288 times a day — the same noise budget `DJB_COOLDOWN_S` exists for. A successful update gets a
    word that carries the new sha, so it is never suppressed.
    """
    seen = state.get("deploy")
    seen = seen if isinstance(seen, dict) else {}
    if (seen.get("word") == word and _is_num(seen.get("at"))
            and now - float(seen["at"]) < DEPLOY_COOLDOWN_S):
        return
    state["deploy"] = {"word": word, "at": now}
    log(msg)


def deploy_update(state: dict, now: float) -> str:
    """Fast-forward the checkout this script runs FROM to its upstream, once per pass (issue #143).

    A merge into djbclark-ade does not reach a launchd job: the plist runs a tracked file in the
    shared checkout, so the job keeps executing whatever that checkout last pulled. On 2026-10-29 it
    was five commits behind, and the two fixes for "the entity never hears the watcher" sat merged
    and unrun for hours. "Merge and the next tick picks it up" was simply false.

    Fast-forward ONLY, and only when nothing else is going on in that checkout:

    - it is the SHARED checkout, so another session may hold staged work there; a fast-forward that
      rewrote a tracked file under it would be destroying someone's uncommitted work. Untracked
      files are fine — git does not touch them.
    - a merge/rebase/cherry-pick/bisect in progress, or a detached HEAD, means someone is mid-something
      and the checkout is not ours to move.
    - `merge-base --is-ancestor` before `--ff-only`, so local commits (a session that committed here
      and did not push) are reported instead of being merged into or reset away. Nothing here ever
      creates a merge commit, resets, stashes or checks anything out.

    ONE step stays manual, once: the job that existed before this code has no deploy step of its own,
    so the shared checkout needs a single `git pull --ff-only` by hand before this can start
    maintaining itself. After that it is self-sustaining, which is the whole point of it.

    A failure is not fatal: this runs inside the pass whose real job is to speak, and a watcher that
    cannot update itself must still be able to say so.

    Returns a short word for the caller's log/`status`: "" when there was nothing to do.
    """
    if DEPLOY_OFF:
        return ""
    if not (SELF_REPO / ".git").exists():
        _deploy_say(state, "not-a-repo",
                    f"deploy skipped: {SELF_REPO} is not a git checkout", now)
        return "skipped: not-a-repo"
    for marker in GIT_BUSY:
        # `--git-path` is the only correct place to look. In a LINKED worktree `.git` is a FILE, so
        # `SELF_REPO/.git/<marker>` was always false there and the check silently passed; the real
        # location is the per-worktree dir (`<common>/worktrees/<name>/`), which is exactly what
        # `--git-path` reports. It answers absolute in a worktree and relative in a plain checkout.
        # A failure to answer is not fatal: `git merge --ff-only` refuses on an operation in progress
        # on its own, so the marker probe is the early, logged word, not the only guard.
        rc, where, _ = _git(["rev-parse", "--git-path", marker])
        if rc != 0 or not where:
            continue
        path = Path(where)
        if not path.is_absolute():
            path = SELF_REPO / path
        if path.exists():
            _deploy_say(state, f"busy:{marker}",
                        f"deploy skipped: {marker} exists at {path} "
                        "(a git operation is in progress there)", now)
            return f"skipped: {marker} in progress"
    rc, dirty, err = _git(["status", "--porcelain", "--untracked-files=no"])
    if rc != 0:
        _deploy_say(state, "status-failed",
                    f"deploy skipped: git status in {SELF_REPO} failed: {(err or 'no output')[:200]}",
                    now)
        return "skipped: status failed"
    if dirty:
        # Count, never contents: this checkout may hold another session's uncommitted work and the
        # log is a shared file.
        n = len(dirty.splitlines())
        _deploy_say(state, "dirty",
                    f"deploy skipped: {SELF_REPO} has {n} uncommitted path(s); a fast-forward there "
                    "could overwrite someone's work", now)
        return "skipped: checkout has uncommitted changes"
    rc, branch, err = _git(["rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD"])
    if rc != 0 or not branch:
        _deploy_say(state, "no-branch",
                    f"deploy skipped: cannot read the branch of {SELF_REPO}: "
                    f"{(err or 'no output')[:200]}", now)
        return "skipped: no branch"
    if branch == "HEAD":
        _deploy_say(state, "detached",
                    f"deploy skipped: {SELF_REPO} has a detached HEAD; not moving it", now)
        return "skipped: detached HEAD"
    rc, old, err = _git(["rev-parse", "HEAD"])
    if rc != 0 or not old:
        _deploy_say(state, "no-head",
                    f"deploy skipped: cannot read HEAD of {SELF_REPO}: "
                    f"{(err or 'no output')[:200]}", now)
        return "skipped: no HEAD"
    # An explicit refspec, not a bare `fetch origin <branch>` + `FETCH_HEAD`: FETCH_HEAD is shared by
    # every session in this checkout, so another session's fetch could rewrite it between our fetch
    # and our read, and an abbreviated ref name can also be shadowed by a same-named TAG on origin.
    # Naming both sides leaves nothing to interpret.
    remote_ref = f"refs/remotes/origin/{branch}"
    refspec = f"+refs/heads/{branch}:{remote_ref}"
    rc, _, err = _git(["fetch", "--quiet", "origin", refspec], timeout=DEPLOY_TIMEOUT_S)
    if rc != 0:
        # Network, auth or a missing remote: the checkout keeps running what it has, and the log
        # says why it is not moving rather than leaving the staleness unexplained.
        _deploy_say(state, "fetch-failed",
                    f"deploy skipped: git fetch origin {refspec} failed: "
                    f"{(err or 'no output')[:200]}", now)
        return "skipped: fetch failed"
    rc, new, err = _git(["rev-parse", remote_ref])
    if rc != 0 or not new:
        _deploy_say(state, "no-remote-ref",
                    f"deploy skipped: cannot read {remote_ref} after fetching {branch}: "
                    f"{(err or 'no output')[:200]}", now)
        return "skipped: no remote ref"
    if new == old:
        # The common case: already current. Restarting the cooldown here would re-log an unrelated
        # refusal that followed an actual update, so the state is left alone.
        return ""
    rc, _, err = _git(["merge-base", "--is-ancestor", old, new])
    if rc != 0:
        _deploy_say(state, "diverged",
                    f"deploy skipped: {branch} at {old[:10]} has commits origin/{branch} lacks "
                    f"(origin is {new[:10]}); not merging, not resetting", now)
        return "skipped: local commits not on origin"
    # The fetch above can take DEPLOY_TIMEOUT_S, and this checkout is SHARED: a session that checks a
    # different branch out during it would make the merge below fast-forward THAT branch — one this
    # function never validated. Not hypothetical on 2026-10-29: the checkout sat on master while the
    # job pointed at a PR branch, so a switch during the fetch would have moved local master onto the
    # PR head, and the next promoter's `merge --ff-only master` would have refused. The pass flock
    # excludes other passes, never other writers, so the only honest guard is to re-read what we are
    # about to move. This leaves the same microsecond window any human `git pull` has, and no more.
    rc, still, err = _git(["rev-parse", "HEAD"])
    rc_branch, still_branch, _ = _git(["rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD"])
    if rc != 0 or rc_branch != 0 or still != old or still_branch != branch:
        _deploy_say(state, "changed-during-fetch",
                    f"deploy skipped: {SELF_REPO} changed during the fetch "
                    f"({branch}@{old[:10]} -> {still_branch or '?'}@{(still or '?')[:10]}); another "
                    "session is using this checkout, so the fast-forward is not ours to make", now)
        return "skipped: checkout changed during the fetch"
    # `--no-overwrite-ignore` is not decoration: `git merge` UPDATES ignored files by default, so a
    # revision that starts tracking a path this checkout already holds as an ignored local file would
    # silently overwrite it — the status check above sees a clean tree precisely BECAUSE the file is
    # ignored. Ordinary untracked files are untouched by `git merge`; ignored ones are not.
    rc, _, err = _git(["merge", "--ff-only", "--quiet", "--no-overwrite-ignore", new],
                      timeout=DEPLOY_TIMEOUT_S)
    if rc != 0:
        # Either lost the race with a session that touched the checkout between the status check and
        # now, or the revision would overwrite an ignored local file; both are refusals, and git's
        # own message names the path.
        _deploy_say(state, "merge-failed",
                    f"deploy skipped: fast-forward {old[:10]} -> {new[:10]} failed: "
                    f"{(err or 'no output')[:200]}", now)
        return "skipped: fast-forward failed"
    moved = running_sha()
    _deploy_say(state, f"updated:{moved}",
                f"deploy updated {old[:10]} -> {moved} ({branch}); this pass still runs the old code, "
                "the next one runs the new", now)
    return f"updated {old[:10]} -> {moved}"


# --- the open loops ----------------------------------------------------------------------------


def open_mail() -> list[str]:
    if not MAILBOX.is_dir():
        return []
    return [p.name for p in sorted(MAILBOX.glob("*.md"))]


def open_jobs(max_age_h: float = 24.0) -> list[dict]:
    """ACTIONABLE job records owned by THIS entity: recent, unfinished, and not someone else's.

    Two filters, each earned from a real false-positive run on 2026-10-24:

    1. **Recency.** An unclosed record from days ago is litter, not a signal: 622 records and 761
       `.done` files exist here, nearly all historical (bigteam records are closed by whoever reads
       them, so "unclosed" is the norm). Without a cutoff the first run reported 145 items and was
       useless noise — the opposite of a quiet watcher.
    2. **Ownership.** Other sessions own their own slices, and their unclosed records are not this
       entity's to act on. With recency alone it still reported 69; filtering to records this entity
       opened (`RW_OWNER` prefix, matching the mailbox instance label) leaves only its own work.
       Never adopt another live session's record: that is the bigteam ownership rule.

    Two actionable shapes, both recency- and ownership-bounded:
    - **no `done` file** — the slice may still be running, or died without finalizing.
    - **`done` exists** — work finished but the record was never closed (bookkeeping debt).
    """
    found = []
    if not BIGTEAM.is_dir():
        return found
    cutoff = time.time() - max_age_h * 3600
    for path in BIGTEAM.glob("*/jobs/*.json"):
        try:
            rec = json.loads(path.read_text())
            mtime = path.stat().st_mtime
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
        if not isinstance(rec, dict) or "closed" in rec or mtime < cutoff:
            continue  # closed, or historical: not a signal either way
        owner = str(rec.get("owner") or "")
        if OWNER and not owner.startswith(OWNER):
            continue  # another session's slice: not ours to act on
        done = rec.get("done")
        found.append({
            "record": str(path),
            "name": rec.get("name") or path.stem,
            "task": path.parent.parent.name,
            "owner": owner or "(none)",
            "done_exists": bool(done and Path(done).exists()),
            "age_h": round((time.time() - mtime) / 3600, 1),
        })
    return found


def open_prs() -> list[str] | None:
    """Open PRs authored by the operator's own account. Best-effort: offline is not an incident.

    The limit is a page size, not a fact: `gh` truncates silently, so the old `--limit 20` made the
    log say "20 open PR(s)" whether there were 20 or 40. A truncated listing that reports itself as
    a count is the same class of lie as calling `accepted` "delivered" — it reads as precision and
    is not. At the cap, an extra marker entry says so and `pr_phrase` prints "at least N".

    A FAILED read returns None, never `[]`: "gh is offline" and "you have no open PRs" are different
    facts, and printing a confident 0 for the first is a count nobody earned.
    """
    rc, out, err = run_split(["gh", "search", "prs", "--author", "@me", "--state", "open",
                              "--json", "number,title", "--limit", str(PR_LIMIT)], timeout=25)
    if rc != 0:
        return None
    try:
        prs = [f"#{p['number']} {p['title']}" for p in json.loads(out)]
    except (ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
        return None
    if len(prs) >= PR_LIMIT:
        prs.append(PR_CAP_MARK)
    return prs


def pr_phrase(prs: list[str] | None) -> str:
    """"at least 100 open PR(s)" when capped, a plain count otherwise, "unknown" when the read failed."""
    if prs is None:
        return "PR count unknown (gh unreadable)"
    capped = bool(prs) and prs[-1] == PR_CAP_MARK
    n = len(prs) - (1 if capped else 0)
    return f"{'at least ' if capped else ''}{n} open PR(s) of yours"


def resolve_pointer() -> Path | None:
    """The file that IS the Tier 1 log for REPO, or None when the pointer itself is unreadable.

    Reads `HOME/.local/state/handoffs/<REPO.name>/main/SESSION_LOG.md` and follows a
    `redirect: <path>` line to the canonical log. The ONE place that decides which file is the
    pointer: `chain_staleness` (the recorded head) and `declared_next_steps` (the open steps) both
    read through it, so they cannot disagree about which log they are talking about. The returned
    path may not exist (a dangling redirect): the caller's own read says so.
    """
    root = HOME / ".local/state/handoffs"
    pointer = root / REPO.name / "main" / "SESSION_LOG.md"
    try:
        text = pointer.read_text()
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError):
        return None
    if m := re.search(r"^redirect:\s*(\S+)", text, re.M):
        return root / m.group(1)
    return pointer


_NEXT_STEPS = re.compile(r"^- Next steps:\s*$")
# Every way a pointer writes a step: `- `, `* `, `+ `, and numbered `1. ` / `1) `. The numbered form
# was excluded at first and that was a MISS of real open work (`graft-pr519-gitignore` lists its two
# remaining steps as `1. `/`2. `), which is the direction that costs the entity: a chain with work
# left says nothing. The count is still structural, never a reading of the words.
_BULLET = re.compile(r"^(\s+)(?:[-*+]|\d+[.)])\s+(.*)$")
# ...except a bullet that says there is NOTHING to do. `- Next steps:` + `  - None` is how a seat
# writes "I am done", and counting it would nudge that seat every cooldown forever with no ack that
# could end it: 12 of 135 real chains said it (2 of them live repo-keyed pointers) when this was
# found. The list is deliberately narrow (the words that mean "no work"), never a guess at intent.
_NOTHING_STEP = re.compile(
    r"^(?:none|nothing|n/?a|no\s+(?:further|more|action|open|pending|remaining|mandatory|merge|"
    r"steps?|work))\b", re.I)
_EMPHASIS = "*`_~ "


def _step_text(raw: str) -> str:
    """A bullet's text with the markdown emphasis a pointer wraps around it removed."""
    return raw.strip().strip(_EMPHASIS)


def declared_next_steps() -> int:
    """How many OPEN next steps the Tier 1 log declares, or 0. The COUNT only, never their text.

    Finds the line `- Next steps:` (column 0, nothing after the colon: how the pointer files write
    it inside `## Current State`) and counts the indented bullets that follow it. The block ends at
    the first blank line or the first unindented line (a new top-level bullet such as
    `- Substrate question answered: ...`, or the next `## ` heading). Two shapes continue a block
    without being a step: a wrapped line of text (indented, no bullet) and a bullet nested deeper
    than the first one. Only bullets at the first bullet's own indentation count, and a bullet whose
    text says there is nothing to do (`- None`, `NOTHING IS PENDING ...`) counts nothing: "done" is
    not a step, and a false 1 here nags the seat forever.

    A missing pointer, an unreadable file, no such section or an empty one is 0: the caller treats
    that as "nothing declared", never as an error and never as a guess.
    """
    path = resolve_pointer()
    if path is None:
        return 0
    try:
        lines = path.read_text().splitlines()
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError):
        return 0
    start = next((i for i, ln in enumerate(lines) if _NEXT_STEPS.match(ln)), None)
    if start is None:
        return 0
    count = 0
    indent: str | None = None
    for ln in lines[start + 1:]:
        if not ln.strip() or not ln[0].isspace():
            break
        m = _BULLET.match(ln)
        if not m or (indent is not None and m.group(1) != indent):
            continue          # wrapped text, or a deeper bullet: part of the block, not a step
        indent = m.group(1)
        text = _step_text(m.group(2))
        if not text or _NOTHING_STEP.match(text) or text[:3].lower() == "[x]":
            continue          # "nothing to do", an empty bullet, or a ticked-off checklist row
        count += 1
    return count


def chain_staleness() -> str | None:
    """The Tier 1 log's recorded head vs the repo's real HEAD. A string when they disagree.

    Reads the pointer, follows `redirect:` to the canonical log, and compares BY ANCESTRY, not
    equality: the pointer is written at session boundaries, so `HEAD` moving ahead of it (the owner
    or a peer seat committing) is the normal case, not staleness. This is the same check the
    `session-handoff` skill documents, done mechanically so nobody has to remember it.
    """
    pointer = resolve_pointer()
    if pointer is None:
        return None
    try:
        text = pointer.read_text()
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError):
        return None
    m = re.search(r"head_sha:\s*([0-9a-f]{7,40})", text)
    if not m:
        return None
    recorded = m.group(1)
    # stdout alone: a git notice on stderr (dubious ownership, safe.directory, a detached-worktree
    # line) would otherwise become part of the sha, and `head[:12]` would never match again — a
    # permanent bogus "stale chain log" report from one warning.
    rc, head, _ = run_split(["git", "-C", str(REPO), "rev-parse", "HEAD"])
    if rc != 0 or not head:
        return None
    # A `git` that could not answer at all (rc 127 from run_split's timeout/OSError catch, or any
    # other rc git does not define for these two commands) is NOT evidence of anything. Saying
    # "not in this repo" or "repo is behind the pointer" on one would be the same cry-wolf defect
    # this function was fixed for, one level down, so those stay silent like every other abort
    # above. `cat-file -e` answers 128 for "no such object", `merge-base --is-ancestor` answers 1
    # for "not an ancestor"; those are the only two codes either command means.
    rc, _, _ = run_split(["git", "-C", str(REPO), "cat-file", "-e", recorded + "^{commit}"])
    if rc == 128:
        return f"Tier 1 log records {recorded[:12]}, repo is at {head[:12]}: not in this repo"
    if rc != 0:
        return None
    rc, _, _ = run_split(["git", "-C", str(REPO), "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", recorded, head])
    if rc == 1:
        return f"Tier 1 log records {recorded[:12]}, repo is at {head[:12]}: repo is behind the pointer"
    return None                       # 0: HEAD or an ancestor; anything else: git could not answer


def survey() -> dict:
    """One pass over every open loop. Pure reads; no model, and it writes nothing it watches."""
    return {
        "mail": open_mail(),
        "jobs": open_jobs(),
        "prs": open_prs(),
        "stale": chain_staleness(),
    }


# --- speaking -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def live_sessions() -> dict[str, dict]:
    """Every session `fleet.py` currently knows, keyed by 8-char paseo id.

    Cached for the life of the process: one pass resolves one target, and fleet.py costs ~3 s.
    An unreadable fleet is an EMPTY map, which makes every candidate fail verification rather
    than passing unchecked — the safe direction for a watcher that must not shout into the void.
    """
    global _LIVE_CACHE
    if _LIVE_CACHE is not None:
        return _LIVE_CACHE
    global _LIVE_ERR
    out: dict[str, dict] = {}
    fleet = HOME / "src/djbclark-ade/skills/session-finder/fleet.py"
    if not fleet.is_file():
        _LIVE_ERR = f"no fleet.py at {fleet}"
    else:
        rc, text, err = run_split(["python3", "-I", str(fleet), "--json"], timeout=90)
        rows: object = []
        parsed = False
        if rc == 0:
            try:
                rows = json.loads(text)
                parsed = True
            except ValueError:
                rows = []
        if rc != 0:
            # Empty is still the safe direction for SENDING (nothing goes out unverified), but it is
            # NOT the same fact as "every seat is dead", and the operator message must not confuse
            # them: telling him to start a seat because fleet.py timed out is false advice.
            _LIVE_ERR = f"fleet.py unreadable (rc {rc}): {(err or text)[:120]}"
        elif not parsed or not isinstance(rows, list):
            # rc 0 is not proof of a reading. fleet.py prints `messaging_warning()` on stderr on its
            # SUCCESSFUL path, and a caller that glued the streams (or a future output change) makes
            # the body unparseable; `[]` used to be indistinguishable from that, so a BROKEN READ
            # became an EMPTY fleet and paged him "Nothing is listening". A parse failure is a blind
            # watcher, whatever rc says.
            _LIVE_ERR = (f"fleet.py printed no fleet (rc 0, "
                         f"{'unparseable' if not parsed else type(rows).__name__}): "
                         f"{(err or text)[:120]}")
            rows = []
        for row in rows if isinstance(rows, list) else []:
            pid = str(row.get("paseo") or "")
            if pid:
                out[pid[:8]] = row
    _LIVE_CACHE = out
    return out


_LIVE_CACHE: dict[str, dict] | None = None
_LIVE_ERR = ""            # why the fleet read failed, "" when it succeeded (feeds the message)


def usable(ident: str) -> bool:
    """Would relay.py find this id? True when fleet lists it and it is neither over nor archived."""
    row = live_sessions().get(ident[:8])
    if not row:
        return False
    return not row.get("ended") and not row.get("archived")


def owner_candidates() -> Iterator[tuple[str, str]]:
    """(source, agent id) pairs for the live session that owns ROLE, best first.

    A launchd job has no session identity — the plist says so in as many words — so resolving on
    `RW_SESSION`/`PASEO_AGENT_ID` alone made this watcher permanently mute: 870 consecutive
    passes logged `session=False` and not one line ever reached the entity. The target has to be
    *looked up at run time*, in the same places the rest of the toolchain looks it up:

      1. `RW_SESSION` / `PASEO_AGENT_ID` — an explicit override, and what a session running this
         by hand already means; used as given, no verification needed (it IS us).
      2. The comm registry's row for ROLE (`agents/comm/registry.json` -> `paseo`), which the
         owning seat keeps current; this is authoritative between the agent and its siblings.
      3. `peer_mail.py resolve --as ROLE --json` -> `live_paseo_ids`, the same record read
         through the peer-comms tool, which prunes ids it can no longer reach.
      4. A saved auto-seat candidate, as a fallback while the registry catches up.
    """
    seen: set[str] = set()
    for src in ("RW_SESSION", "PASEO_AGENT_ID"):
        val = (os.environ.get(src) or "").strip()
        if val:
            seen.add(val[:8])
            yield (src, val)
    try:
        reg = json.loads((REPO / "agents/comm/registry.json").read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        reg = {}
    row = reg.get(ROLE) if isinstance(reg, dict) else None
    paseo = str((row or {}).get("paseo") or "") if isinstance(row, dict) else ""
    if paseo and paseo[:8] not in seen:
        seen.add(paseo[:8])
        yield ("registry", paseo)
    mail = REPO / "projects/hiwymi/spec/composite-individual/tools/peer-comms/peer_mail.py"
    if mail.is_file():
        rc, out, _ = run_split(["python3", "-I", str(mail), "resolve", "--as", ROLE, "--json"],
                               timeout=60)
        try:
            ids = json.loads(out).get("live_paseo_ids") or [] if rc == 0 else []
        except (ValueError, AttributeError):
            ids = []
        for ident in ids if isinstance(ids, list) else []:
            ident = str(ident)
            if ident[:8] not in seen:
                seen.add(ident[:8])
                yield ("peer_mail", ident)
    if AUTO_SEAT and (candidate := auto_seat_candidate(load_state())) and candidate[:8] not in seen:
        # Do not depend on the new seat rewriting the peer registry before the next pass.
        yield ("auto-seat", candidate)


def owner_session() -> tuple[str, str]:
    """(agent id, source) for this role, or ("", reason) when nothing live resolves."""
    first = ""
    for src, ident in owner_candidates():
        if src in ("RW_SESSION", "PASEO_AGENT_ID"):
            return ident, src
        first = first or f"{src}={ident[:8]}"
        if usable(ident):
            return ident, src
    if _LIVE_ERR:
        return "", (f"cannot check which session is live: {_LIVE_ERR} — this is a broken READ, not "
                    f"a dead seat ({ROLE!r} registry/peer_mail said {first or 'nothing'})")
    return "", (f"no live session for {ROLE!r} (registry/peer_mail gave {first or 'nothing'})")


def why_blocked(ident: str) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """(state, why) for a target that just refused the line: `relay.py --why <id> --json`.

    "Busy" is three different things and only one of them is healthy (refinement of the same djbclark
    rule the `--why` flag itself came from, 2026-10-29 "be able to tell why a message can't be
    delivered yet (eg picklist vs tab hung etc)"):

    - `working` — mid-turn. Healthy; the entity is listening to its own work, not dark.
    - `card:question|permission|plan` — waiting on a HUMAN. The seat is dark by construction, and it
      stays dark until someone answers. Exactly the silence this watcher exists to prevent.
    - `silent` — running with nothing new for 15 min: a hung turn.

    With auto-seat enabled, a missing diagnosis for a UUID gets one direct inspect (up to 20 s).
    Only an explicit AGENT_NOT_FOUND proves absence; a blind read remains `unknown`.
    """
    uuid = re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-fA-F]{8}(?:-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}){3}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12}", ident)

    def unknown(reason: str) -> tuple[str, str]:
        if AUTO_SEAT and uuid:
            rc, out, err = run_split(["paseo", "agent", "inspect", "--json", "--", ident],
                                     timeout=20)
            try:
                info = json.loads(out or err)
            except ValueError:
                info = None
            error = info.get("error") if isinstance(info, dict) else None
            if rc in (0, 1) and isinstance(error, dict) and error.get("code") == "AGENT_NOT_FOUND":
                return "gone", "direct inspect confirmed AGENT_NOT_FOUND"
        return "unknown", reason

    relay = HOME / "src/djbclark-ade/skills/session-finder/relay.py"
    if not relay.is_file():
        return unknown(f"no relay.py at {relay}")
    rc, out, err = run_split(["python3", "-I", str(relay), "--why", ident, "--json"], timeout=90)
    try:
        d = json.loads(out)
    except ValueError:
        return unknown(f"unreadable --why (rc {rc}): {(err or out)[:160]}")
    if rc not in (0, 1) or not isinstance(d, dict) or not d.get("state") or d.get("state") == "unknown":
        return unknown(f"no diagnosis in --why (rc {rc}): {out[:160]}")
    if d["state"] == "gone" and not uuid:
        return "unknown", "absence not proven for a non-UUID id"
    return str(d["state"]), str(d.get("why") or "")


def relay_word(out: str) -> str:
    """relay.py's OWN word from a NON-JSON send, read from its status line and nothing else.

    Only reached when relay.py answered without a `--json` report (an older relay.py), so this is a
    fallback, not the contract: the send asks for machine output (see `relay_report`). rc==0 prints
    exactly one of `delivered` / `accepted` / `queued` after "via <route>: ". The rest of the output
    can carry a peer's untrusted reply preview, and a reply that happens to contain ": queued" must
    not decide what this watcher logs about its own send. Anything unrecognised is `sent`, never
    `delivered`: the strongest word is the one that starts a log lying.
    """
    own = out.split("its reply (untrusted data", 1)[0]
    if ": queued" in own or own.startswith("queued"):
        return "queued"
    if ": accepted" in own:
        return "accepted"
    if ": delivered" in own:
        return "delivered"
    return "sent"


def relay_report(out: str) -> dict | None:
    """relay.py's `--json` send report (relay.py:3585), or None when it printed none.

    The prose this replaced was a string contract with a tool that moves. relay.py's rc 1 shapes split
    three ways — a REFUSAL (nothing sent, retry is right), an UNVERIFIED send ("it may have reached the
    daemon", "do not resend": retrying is how the seat hears the same line twice) and FAILED — and
    reading that off prose drifts in BOTH directions: a reworded unverified shape gets retried
    forever, a reworded refusal is never explained and a card-dark seat is never paged. relay.py
    already prints `{"result": ..., "state": "queued", "reason": ...}` for exactly this.
    """
    try:
        d = json.loads(out)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    if not isinstance(d, dict):
        return None
    return d if ("result" in d or "state" in d or "queued" in d) else None


def report_word(report: dict, rc: int) -> str:
    """The outcome word from relay.py's JSON report: its own `result`, or `state: queued`."""
    if report.get("queued") is True or report.get("state") == "queued":
        return "queued"                     # defer_send's shape; `result` is not in it
    word = str(report.get("result") or "")
    if word in SENT_WORDS or word == "unverified":
        return word
    # A report with no word we know. On rc 0 something was sent (say the weakest true thing); on a
    # non-zero rc the honest reading is "taken without proof", which is settled and never resent.
    return "sent" if rc == 0 else "unverified"


def send_argv(relay: Path, ident: str, text: str) -> list[str]:
    """The send's OWN argument list, in one place: a test can pin the flags `to_session` depends on.

    `--json` is not decoration: without it relay.py prints prose, `relay_report` gets nothing, and the
    caller cannot tell a delivery from a refusal. `--no-ack` is why a launchd sender (no Paseo
    identity) gets the plain send rather than relay's ack dance. Kept as its own function because
    asserting these flags anywhere else in the file — relay.py's own source is full of the same
    string — proves nothing about what this watcher actually passes.
    """
    return ["python3", "-I", str(relay), ident, text, "--no-ack", "--json"]


def to_session(text: str) -> str:
    """One line into the owner session; returns the OUTCOME, not a bare bool.

    `delivered`, `queued`, `working` (mid-turn, healthy), `card:<kind>` (waiting on a human),
    `silent` (a hung turn), `unverified` (relay took it without proof: do NOT resend),
    `unreachable` (no live session) or `FAILED`. The caller logs it and `cmd_pass` decides what a
    failure means; a bool could not tell "the seat is gone" from "the seat is working", and that
    distinction is the whole point of the change. The three busy words come
    from `why_blocked`: a seat parked on an unanswered prompt and a seat that is quietly wedged both
    used to read as the single healthy word `skipped`, which is the entity going dark while the
    watcher says everything is fine.

    NO `--idle`. It looks right and is wrong: `--idle S` BLOCKS the deferral (relay.py's defer_wanted
    requires `a.idle is None`), so a target that is busy when the wait expires gets a refusal and no
    queue record — the line is dropped and the pass burns its interval waiting to drop it.

    What actually happens without it, proven under a real launchd job: an idle target takes the line
    now (`delivered`); a busy one is REFUSED and cannot be deferred, because deferral needs an
    identifiable sender and a launchd job has no Paseo id and no fleet `self` row. A session running
    this by hand (`RW_SESSION` set) does get the queued path, since it has an identity. The outcome is
    LOGGED either way: a silently mute loudspeaker is the failure this whole script exists to prevent.
    """
    ident, why = owner_session()
    if not ident:
        log(f"cannot speak to session: {why}")
        return "unreachable"
    relay = HOME / "src/djbclark-ade/skills/session-finder/relay.py"
    if not relay.is_file():
        log(f"cannot speak to session: no relay.py at {relay}")
        return "unreachable"
    rc, out, err = run_split(send_argv(relay, ident, text), timeout=320)
    report = relay_report(out)
    if report is not None:
        word = report_word(report, rc)
    elif rc == 0:
        # An older relay.py that does not answer `--json`: its status line is all there is.
        word = relay_word(out)
    elif any(marker in (out + "\n" + err).lower() for marker in MAYBE_SENT):
        # No machine report, but relay.py's own "it may have arrived / do not resend" line: settled,
        # never retried. Checked BEFORE asking why, because a seat that is `working` when the line
        # arrives unverified must not be handed it again.
        word = "unverified"
    else:
        # rc 1 with no report: relay.py refused, and WHICH refusal it is decides whether the seat is
        # dark — so ask, never match prose. A refusal that is really a card or a hung turn is an
        # outage; a reworded refusal must not blind the pager (`skipped` is only the fallback).
        state = why_blocked(ident)[0] if rc == 1 else "unknown"
        if state.startswith("card:") or state in ("working", "silent"):
            word = state
        elif any(marker in (out + "\n" + err) for marker in CARD_REFUSAL):
            # The refusal's OWN words say "blocked on a card" and `--why` could not name the kind:
            # still darkness, not the healthy `skipped` this used to be filed as.
            word = "card:unknown"
        elif "not sent" in (out + "\n" + err) or "cannot take it now" in (out + "\n" + err):
            word = "skipped"
        else:
            word = "FAILED"
        # `why_blocked`'s second element is NOT logged. For a card it is relay.py's
        # `paseo_card_detail`: the agent's own tool input, which relay.py documents as "shown by
        # `--why` only, never filed on a record or logged" — a permission prompt can carry a command
        # with a token in it. The outcome word is what this log needs.
    log(f"relay to {ident[:8]} (via {why}): {word}" + (f" rc={rc}: {out[:200]}" if rc else ""))
    return word


def to_djbclark(text: str) -> bool:
    """The quiet topic. Resolved through the registry, never a hard-coded thread id.

    stdout ALONE for the route: `inbox-target` warns on stderr and still exits 0 ("unknown name ->
    INBOX_DEFAULT + stderr warning"), and this is the paging channel — a warning glued onto the
    target would send to a destination that does not exist, on the one path whose failure means
    djbclark never hears that the seat is dark.
    """
    target = ""
    reg = HOME / "ops/site-djbclark/bin/inbox-target"
    if reg.is_file():
        rc, out, _ = run_split([str(reg), "dann"], timeout=15)
        target = out if rc == 0 else ""
    target = target or os.environ.get("RW_DJB_TARGET", "")
    if not target:
        return False
    rc, _ = run(["hermes", "send", "-t", target, text], timeout=30)
    return rc == 0


def should_tell_djbclark(item: str, state: dict, now: float) -> bool:
    """Anti-noise: is this item past its cooldown? A CHECK — this stamps nothing.

    The stamp used to be written HERE, before `to_djbclark()` ran, so one failed `hermes send` (or an
    unresolvable `inbox-target`) silenced that page for the whole cooldown with nothing retrying it:
    the one channel whose job is telling him the entity is dark could lose exactly that page to a
    transient blip. `told` now records a page he ACTUALLY received — see `djb_page_sent`, which every
    page site calls only on a successful send.
    """
    told = state.get("told")
    if not isinstance(told, dict):
        told = state["told"] = {}
    last = told.get(item)
    return not (last and now - float(last) < DJB_COOLDOWN_S)


def djb_page_sent(item: str, state: dict, now: float) -> None:
    """He received it: start the cooldown. Called ONLY when `to_djbclark` returned True."""
    told = state.get("told")
    if not isinstance(told, dict):
        told = state["told"] = {}
    told[item] = now


def nudge_due(ident: str, state: dict, now: float) -> bool:
    """May the idle-with-work nudge be considered this pass? Switch on, a seat to speak to, cooldown over.

    The window is `0 <= age < NUDGE_S`, not `age < NUDGE_S`: a stamp in the FUTURE (a hand edit, or a
    clock that ran ahead when the stamp was written and was corrected later) must expire rather than
    hold the cooldown open for as long as it takes the clock to catch up — the one shape that could
    silence the line that keeps this entity alive, permanently.
    """
    if not NUDGE_ON or not ident:
        return False
    nudged = state.get("nudged")
    last = nudged.get(KEY_NEXT) if isinstance(nudged, dict) else None
    return not (last is not None and 0 <= now - float(last) < NUDGE_S)


def nudge_sent(state: dict, now: float) -> None:
    """The nudge reached the seat: start the cooldown. Called ONLY after a send that settled, never
    before it (the `djb_page_sent` lesson: stamping first lets one transient failure silence the
    nudge for the whole cooldown)."""
    nudged = state.get("nudged")
    if not isinstance(nudged, dict):
        nudged = state["nudged"] = {}
    nudged[KEY_NEXT] = now


# A refused pass is a turn boundary, not an outage: the seat is usually mid-work. Only the words
# that mean "this will not end by itself" are darkness, and only after they repeat this many passes.
SEAT_STUCK_PASSES = _env_int("RW_STUCK_PASSES", 3)

# A line the seat refused is kept and retried, not dropped (see `_pass`). Cap so a seat that stays
# busy for a day cannot grow the state file without bound; the newest lines are the ones that matter.
PENDING_MAX = _env_int("RW_PENDING_MAX", 6)
# Keys of the state-like pass items (see `_pass`): a `pending` entry carrying one is dropped at retry
# time when this pass no longer recomputes the condition.
KEY_STALE = "stale chain log"
KEY_STUCK = "job(s) finished but not closed"
KEY_NEXT = "next move"

# The idle-with-work nudge (see `_pass`). Its own cooldown, in its own `state["nudged"]` map: it is a
# line to the SEAT, so it shares nothing with the per-item budget of the pages to djbclark.
# RW_IDLE_NUDGE=0 turns it off.
NUDGE_ON = os.environ.get("RW_IDLE_NUDGE", "1").strip().lower() not in ("0", "false", "no", "off")
NUDGE_S = _env_int("RW_IDLE_NUDGE_S", 3 * 3600)

# A mechanism that starts agents is opt-in until it has run in the field.
AUTO_SEAT = _env_int("RW_AUTO_SEAT", 0, minimum=0)
AUTO_SEAT_S = _env_int("RW_AUTO_SEAT_S", 3600)
AUTO_SEAT_PROVIDER = os.environ.get("RW_AUTO_SEAT_PROVIDER", "")


def auto_seat_candidate(state: dict) -> str:
    """The most recent run's id, including an unconfirmed one. This is NOT a cooldown stamp.

    Reuse the existing `told` bookkeeping map (numeric values, sanitized by load_state), with a
    separate namespace from pages/refusals. Keep it after confirmation so a stale peer registry
    cannot cause a second seat after cooldown. A failed confirmation is a failed READ of this id,
    never permission to spawn again; a later probe must prove THIS candidate gone first.
    """
    prefix = f"auto-seat candidate:{ROLE}:"
    candidates = [(float(at), key[len(prefix):]) for key, at in state.get("told", {}).items()
                  if key.startswith(prefix) and key[len(prefix):] and _is_num(at)]
    return max(candidates, default=(0, ""))[1]


def open_desktop_tab(agent_id: str) -> str:
    """Give a started seat a Paseo Desktop tab; return "" on success, else a reason to LOG.

    A `paseo run` agent has NO tab (tabs are client-side; only `paseo agent open` or the UI make
    one), so on 2026-10-30 a lead handed over that way was invisible for 13 h with a question card
    nobody could see. `auto_seat` never answers a `card:*` — that is djbclark's — so an
    auto-started seat that hits one would be alive, reachable by relay and UNSEEN: the exact
    darkness this file exists to end, one level down. djbclark ruled 2026-10-30 that the start
    opens the tab and LOGS a failure; unlike `self-slash --finish --successor` it must not fail the
    start, because there is no old agent to fall back to and the seat is reachable either way.

    `agent open` returns as soon as the app is launched with the deep link and cannot confirm a tab
    appeared (#159), so "" means only "the CLI accepted it" and callers must not log that a tab
    EXISTS (adversary N1, 2026-10-30: a launchd pass with the app not running can send a deep link
    that is dropped). RW_AUTO_SEAT_NO_OPEN (`1`/`true`/`yes`/`on`, case-insensitive) skips the
    attempt on a host with no desktop and still returns a line naming the hand-run fix. This
    file's other switches each invented their own rule (`RW_IDLE_NUDGE` treats anything but
    0/false/no/off as on, `RW_NO_DEPLOY` treats `off` as on), so this one is an explicit
    allowlist rather than a claim to match them (adversary D5, 2026-10-30).
    """
    switch = os.environ.get("RW_AUTO_SEAT_NO_OPEN", "").strip()
    if switch.lower() in ("1", "true", "yes", "on"):
        return f"NOT opened (RW_AUTO_SEAT_NO_OPEN={switch}): run paseo agent open {agent_id}"
    rc, out, err = run_split(["paseo", "agent", "open", agent_id, "--json"], timeout=20)
    if rc == 0:
        return ""
    detail = " ".join((err or out).split())
    return (f"NOT opened in Paseo Desktop (rc={rc}{': ' + detail[:160] if detail else ''}); "
            f"it is reachable by relay but has no tab: run paseo agent open {agent_id}")


def tab_page(agent_id: str, reason: str) -> str:
    """The page for a seat that is alive and reachable but has no Paseo Desktop tab."""
    return (f"Dann: {ROLE!r}'s auto-started seat {agent_id} has no Paseo "
            f"Desktop tab, so a question card on it would be unseen. {reason}")


def tab_once(agent_id: str, state: dict, now: float) -> str:
    """Open an ADOPTED auto-seat agent's tab ONCE; "" when nothing is owed or the open succeeded.

    A start can create the seat and then lose its own confirmation (a dropped response, or a
    transient inspect error), and `auto_seat` returns before its open; the recovery path then
    adopts that agent from `paseo ls` on a later pass and calls it healthy. Nothing ever gave it a
    tab, so it is exactly as invisible as an unopened fresh seat — Codex's P2 and the adversary's
    N4 on 2026-10-30 found the same hole independently. One attempt per id, whatever the outcome,
    so a host with no desktop cannot re-run this every pass.

    The attempt is stamped AFTER the open, like the seat itself, so a kill DURING the open leaves
    it unrecorded and the next pass retries (`agent open` is idempotent; a never-opened seat is not
    recoverable). It is NOT a start stamp: it never opens a cooldown or counts as a seat.
    """
    key = f"auto-seat tab:{agent_id}"
    told = state.setdefault("told", {})
    if key in told:
        return ""
    reason = open_desktop_tab(agent_id)
    told[key] = now
    save_state(state)
    log(f"auto-seat: adopted {agent_id}: " + (reason or
        "sent it to Paseo Desktop (a tab is not confirmable from here)"))
    return reason


def auto_seat(word: str, state: dict, now: float, note: dict | None = None) -> str:
    """Start only a PROVEN ABSENT seat; return the confirmed id, otherwise ''.

    `note`, when given, receives `note["tab"]` with the reason the started seat could NOT be given
    a Paseo Desktop tab, so the caller can put it in the page to djbclark: he is the one person who
    must see a `card:*`, and a seat he cannot see is the darkness this file exists to end.

    Diagnosis       Start?  Reason
    gone            yes     Inspect-confirmed absent (UUID id, not a blind read).
    error           no      Inspect could not answer: broken READ, daemon/socket may be down.
    refused         no      Finished/archived session or non-idle/running Paseo state.
    unknown         no      No diagnosis; absence not proven.
    silent/working  no      A seat EXISTS; a second one would duplicate the individual.
    card:*          NEVER   His to answer; spawning leaves the card AND adds another seat.
    idle            no      Healthy; the declared-next-step nudge covers it.

    `refused`/`unknown` never auto-start; when no owner resolves, they still page the human.

    We are narrowing djbclark's 2026-10-30 "including after a hard error" ruling for one case:
    `error` means a broken READ, not a dead seat, and `paseo run` will fail in that same state.
    He can overrule it. `silent` also refuses because the hung seat still exists.
    """
    if not AUTO_SEAT:
        return ""

    def refuse(reason: str, message: str = "") -> str:
        # Same shape as _deploy_say, independent of the successful-start cooldown. ONE refusal
        # per role/window even if the reason changes. Only pre-invocation refusals use this.
        key = f"auto-seat refusal:{ROLE}"
        told = state.setdefault("told", {})
        stamp = told.get(key)
        if not (_is_num(stamp) and 0 <= now - float(stamp) < AUTO_SEAT_S):
            told[key] = now
            log(message or f"auto-seat: not started ({reason})")
        return ""

    told = state.setdefault("told", {})
    unresolved_key = f"auto-seat unresolved:{ROLE}"
    if unresolved_key in told:
        # Observed 2026-10-30: `paseo ls --json --background` returned rc 0 and a plain
        # JSON array of non-archived agents with id, shortId, name, status, cwd (lowercase).
        # No --all (archived agents) or --cwd (unsupported). Listing may ONLY adopt/clear;
        # it never proves absence for a start, even when the attempt window has expired.
        rc, out, err = run_split(["paseo", "ls", "--json", "--background"], timeout=20)
        reason = f"listing rc={rc}"
        rows = None
        if rc == 0:
            try:
                rows = json.loads(out)
                reason = "listing is not an agent array"
            except ValueError:
                reason = "unreadable listing JSON"
        if isinstance(rows, list) and all(isinstance(row, dict) for row in rows):
            matches = [row for row in rows if row.get("name") == f"{ROLE} (auto-seat)"]
            reason = f"{len(matches)} matching agents"
            recovered = matches[0].get("id") if len(matches) == 1 else ""
            if len(matches) <= 1 and (not matches or isinstance(recovered, str) and recovered.strip()):
                if matches:
                    told[f"auto-seat candidate:{ROLE}:{recovered}"] = now
                del told[unresolved_key]
                save_state(state)
                log(f"auto-seat: recovered lost id {recovered}" if matches else
                    "auto-seat: unresolved cleared (no live auto-seat agent)")
                return ""  # Diagnose a recovered candidate, or prove absence, on the NEXT pass.
            if len(matches) == 1:
                reason = "matching agent has no usable id"
        return refuse("unresolved start", f"auto-seat: unresolved start not reconciled ({reason})")

    if word != "gone":
        return refuse(f"{word or 'unreadable'}: absence not proven")
    seated = state.setdefault("seated", {})
    last = seated.get(ROLE)
    # Future stamps are IGNORED: a hand edit or clock correction must not silence starts forever.
    if _is_num(last) and 0 <= now - float(last) < AUTO_SEAT_S:
        return refuse("cooldown")
    attempt_key = f"auto-seat attempt:{ROLE}"
    told = state.setdefault("told", {})
    last_attempt = told.get(attempt_key)
    if _is_num(last_attempt) and 0 <= now - float(last_attempt) < AUTO_SEAT_S:
        return refuse("attempt window")
    if not AUTO_SEAT_PROVIDER.strip():
        return refuse("RW_AUTO_SEAT_PROVIDER unset")
    pointer = resolve_pointer()
    pointer = pointer.resolve() if pointer is not None else None
    exists = pointer is not None and pointer.is_file()
    if not exists:
        log("auto-seat: no pointer")
    prompt = (
        f"Resume the existing individual as role {ROLE} in repo {REPO.resolve()}.\n"
        + (f"Tier 1 pointer file: {pointer}\n" if exists else
           f"No Tier 1 pointer file exists ({pointer or 'none'}); resume from the repo's state.\n")
        + "When available, read that file's ## Current State and - Next steps: and continue at the first unstarted step.\n"
        "Do not page djbclark; if the work needs his decision, put it in the report.\n"
        f"This seat was started automatically after the previous one disappeared at "
        f"{time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z', time.localtime(now))} (trigger: {word}); "
        "it stopped answering.\n"
    )
    # addRunOptions in the installed Paseo bundle supplies provider/no-wait/cwd/title;
    # addJsonAndDaemonHostOptions supplies --json, agentRunSchema.idField supplies agentId.
    # No model/thinking/default-provider: the provider is operator configuration, forwarded as-is.
    # `env -u PASEO_AGENT_ID`: `paseo run` parents the new agent to $PASEO_AGENT_ID when the
    # variable is set, so a pass run BY HAND from inside an agent would make the entity's next seat
    # that agent's SUBAGENT (measured 2026-10-30: the probe came back ParentAgentId = the calling
    # seat). launchd never sets it, but a debugging pass does; `bin/self-slash` clears it for the
    # same reason (its header, 2026-10-30).
    argv = ["env", "-u", "PASEO_AGENT_ID",
            "paseo", "run", "--provider", AUTO_SEAT_PROVIDER, "--no-wait", "--json",
            "--cwd", str(REPO.resolve()), "--title", f"{ROLE} (auto-seat)", "--", prompt]
    deadline = time.monotonic() + 120
    # An invoked start can have created an agent even when its response is lost. Persist the
    # attempt before invoking it; no outcome may permit another invocation inside this window.
    told[attempt_key] = now
    told[unresolved_key] = now
    save_state(state)
    rc, out, err = run_split(argv, timeout=100)
    try:
        result = json.loads(out)
    except ValueError:
        result = None
    new_id = result.get("agentId") if isinstance(result, dict) else None
    new_id = new_id.strip() if isinstance(new_id, str) else ""
    outcome = ("failed" if rc != 0 else "unreadable JSON" if result is None else
               "no agentId" if not new_id else "id returned")
    log(f"auto-seat: attempted paseo run rc={rc}; stderr={err[:160]!r}; "
        f"id={new_id or 'none'}; {outcome}")
    if not new_id:
        return ""
    # The daemon may have created a seat even if the following inspect fails. Retain its id BEFORE
    # that read, without stamping seated; the next pass must diagnose it instead of duplicating it.
    prefix = f"auto-seat candidate:{ROLE}:"
    for key in list(told):
        if key.startswith(prefix):
            del told[key]
    told[prefix + new_id] = now
    save_state(state)
    if rc != 0:
        return ""
    remaining = min(20, deadline - time.monotonic())
    if remaining <= 0:
        log(f"auto-seat: confirmation failed id={new_id}; rc=not-invoked; deadline exhausted")
        return ""
    rc, out, err = run_split(["paseo", "agent", "inspect", "--json", "--", new_id],
                           timeout=remaining)
    try:
        info = json.loads(out) if rc == 0 else None
        # Inspect serializes structured agent data; CLI versions may wrap it in a list.
        info = info[0] if isinstance(info, list) and info else info
    except ValueError:
        info = None
    if not (isinstance(info, dict) and info.get("Id") == new_id
            and info.get("Archived") is False
            and info.get("Status") in ("idle", "running", "initializing")):
        log(f"auto-seat: confirmation failed id={new_id}; rc={rc}; stderr={err[:160]!r}; "
            "new agent could not be confirmed addressable")
        return ""
    seated[ROLE] = now                         # ONLY after inspect confirmed it, like djb_page_sent
    del told[unresolved_key]
    save_state(state)                          # persist before a potentially slow paging operation
    log(f"auto-seat: started {new_id} (was {word}) — {pointer if exists else 'none'}")
    # The seat exists, is reachable and is NOT visible to the human until it has a tab. Logged
    # AFTER the state that records it: a kill during the open must leave a seated, diagnosable id.
    tab = open_desktop_tab(new_id)
    # Attempted, whatever the outcome: `tab_once` must not re-send this id when the recovery path
    # adopts it later. Saved after the open so a kill DURING it leaves the attempt unrecorded and
    # the adoption retries it — `agent open` is idempotent, a never-opened seat is not recoverable.
    told[f"auto-seat tab:{new_id}"] = now
    save_state(state)
    log(f"auto-seat: " + (tab or
        f"sent {new_id} to Paseo Desktop (a tab is not confirmable from here)"))
    if tab and note is not None:
        note["tab"] = tab
    return new_id

# A page size for `gh search prs`, not a ceiling on reality: at the cap the count is a lower bound.
PR_LIMIT = _env_int("RW_PR_LIMIT", 100)
PR_CAP_MARK = f"(at least {PR_LIMIT}: the listing is capped)"

# A card state is a HUMAN waiting, so it is darkness by construction. Two rounds of review found the
# same bug twice here: missing `picker`/`unknown` meant a seat parked on a picker logged
# `card:picker` every pass as ordinary status, reset the streak, and was never paged — dark, reported
# as fine — and round 3 found `card:slash_menu` (relay.py's `pane_look`, an open command menu) missing
# in exactly the same way. So this map is now only the MESSAGE table: `seat_dark` treats any
# `card:*` as dark whether or not it is listed, and a card kind relay.py adds tomorrow cannot be the
# one that stays silent.
DARK_MEANING = {
    "card:question": "a question card is waiting for an answer, so it cannot take text",
    "card:plan": "a plan card is waiting for approval, so it cannot take text",
    "card:permission": "a permission prompt is waiting, so it cannot take text",
    "card:picker": "a picker is waiting for a choice, so it cannot take text",
    "card:slash_menu": "an open command menu is holding the seat, so it cannot take text",
    "card:unknown": "a card the toolchain cannot classify is waiting, so it cannot take text",
    "silent": "it is running with nothing new and is not taking text: a hung turn",
}

# relay.py `--why` states that report the seat as able to take text RIGHT NOW (`deliverable: true`).
# A quiet pass reads one of these every 5 minutes on a seat that is simply idle — a healthy
# observation, which must end a darkness streak. Leaving `idle` out let three card episodes spread
# over a day, with healthy idle passes between them, page him "unable to take text for 3 passes in a
# row": both clauses false, in exactly the dishonesty class this watcher exists to remove.
DELIVERABLE_WHY = frozenset({"idle"})

# A send that reached (or may have reached) the seat: the pending line is settled, not retried.
SENT_WORDS = frozenset({"delivered", "accepted", "queued", "sent"})

# A send the route SETTLED but did not VERIFY. relay.py documents `accepted` as "the daemon took it;
# not a verified delivery", `queued` as waiting for the target to go idle (or for a card to be
# answered), and `sent` as the weakest rc-0 reading. For the pending list they are done — the line
# must not go twice — but as evidence about the SEAT they say nothing, so a pass that gets one ASKS
# `--why` rather than resetting the streak (Codex review, 2026-10-30, P2). Before this, a hung Claude
# Paseo seat that keeps ACCEPTING text cleared its own count on every busy pass: the probe that would
# have called it `silent` runs on a QUIET pass, and a seat that produces a line every pass never has
# one — so the streak could never reach SEAT_STUCK_PASSES and nobody was ever paged.
UNPROVEN_SEND = frozenset(SENT_WORDS - {"delivered"})

# The words that PROVE the channel worked or the seat is healthy — the only ones that reset a
# darkness streak. A FAILED, `skipped`, unverified or merely-settled send says nothing about the seat,
# and letting it reset the count is how a genuinely dark seat hides behind a flaky route (or one
# 5-minute `paseo send` blip wipes out the four passes that were about to page him).
HEALTHY_WORDS = frozenset({"delivered", "working"}) | DELIVERABLE_WHY

# relay.py's rc==1 shapes split three ways and only one may be retried. A REFUSAL says "not sent",
# so nothing arrived and retrying next pass is right. An UNVERIFIED send is the opposite: the route
# took (or may have taken) the message and could not prove it, and relay's own text says "do not
# resend" — resending is how a peer hears the same line twice. FAILED is the remainder (a bad id, a
# usage error): nothing was sent, so a retry is harmless.
MAYBE_SENT = (
    "may have reached the daemon",        # route_paseo: `paseo send` failed for an unknown reason
    "do not resend",                      # PULLED_WHY / RECALLED_WHY / MOVED_WHY / retry_leftover
    "pulled back out of the queue",
    "not shown to be the one we typed",
    "its transcript is not the one",
)

# relay.py's refusal wording when the target waits on a card (its own `CARD_WHY` prefix set). Read ONLY
# when `--why` could not name the kind: the old fallback filed every "not sent" refusal as the healthy
# word `skipped`, so a card-dark seat behind a broken `--why` read as fine. We know it is a card, not
# which kind, and `card:unknown` is exactly what that is.
CARD_REFUSAL = (
    "blocked on a permission or question",
    "blocked on a question or permission prompt",
)


def seat_dark(word: str) -> str:
    """The darkness class of a to_session outcome, or '' when the seat is fine.

    ANY `card:*` is dark, listed or not. `DARK_MEANING` is a copy of another file's vocabulary and
    cannot stay complete: relay.py's `pane_look` gained `card:slash_menu` after this table was
    written, and membership-only made the newest card kind the one that stayed silent — the same
    bug, found twice, in two different keys. A card is a human being waited on, so the prefix is the
    rule and the table is only the wording.

    `working` is a seat doing its job, and `skipped`-for-unknown-reason is a `--why` that could not
    be read: guessing from a blind probe would page him for nothing. `unreachable` is already paged
    by its own branch in `cmd_pass`.
    """
    if word in DARK_MEANING:
        return word
    if word.startswith("card:"):
        return word
    return ""


def escalate_dark(word: str, ident: str, state: dict, now: float) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """(item, message) for a dark seat, or ("", "") when there is nothing to say yet.

    A refusal is not automatically darkness: `working` is a healthy seat mid-turn. But a seat parked
    on a prompt only a human can answer, or wedged with nothing new for 15 min, will not come back on
    its own — and the watcher is the only thing on this machine that can see it. One message per
    cooldown, and only once the state REPEATS: one dark pass is a turn boundary.

    The count is CONSECUTIVE DARKNESS, not a per-class tally. The per-class version kept the hole
    open across two rounds of review: a seat alternating `card:question` and `silent` (a permission
    prompt whose turn times out, then re-prompts) counted to one under each key forever and was
    never paged, though it had been unable to take text the whole time. The class is kept BESIDE the
    count (`stuck_class`) only to word the message; the count itself does not care which darkness it
    was.

    The ITEM comes back with the message so the caller can stamp the cooldown only after a send that
    actually reached him (`djb_page_sent`): stamping here, before the send, is how one failed hermes
    call silenced the dark-seat page for six hours. It is deliberately class-INDEPENDENT: with a
    per-class item, a flipping class re-armed the cooldown and paged him every three passes.

    `ident` stays in the signature for the call sites and is deliberately not part of the count: the
    watcher speaks to ONE role's seat, and an id that changes on every `/new` would restart the
    streak — the same hole as the per-class key, one key over.
    """
    dark = seat_dark(word)
    if not dark:
        if word in HEALTHY_WORDS:
            # The channel worked or the seat is healthy: no streak to keep.
            state["stuck"] = {}
            state.pop("stuck_class", None)
        return "", ""
    prev = state.get("stuck")
    n = 0
    if isinstance(prev, dict):
        raw = prev.get("n")
        if isinstance(raw, int) and not isinstance(raw, bool):
            n = raw
    n += 1
    # `{"n": N}` is the shape `load_state` can vet (`_is_num` on every value), so a hand-edited
    # count is dropped rather than crashing the pass — and the previous head's per-class dict simply
    # reads as `n == 0` here.
    state["stuck"] = {"n": n}
    state["stuck_class"] = dark
    item = "seat dark"
    if n < SEAT_STUCK_PASSES or not should_tell_djbclark(item, state, now):
        return "", ""
    meaning = DARK_MEANING.get(dark) or f"it is parked on `{dark}`, a card kind this table has not learned yet"
    return item, (f"Dann: the {ROLE} seat has been unable to take text for {n} passes in a row "
                  f"(latest: {meaning}). It is not working on anything you can see. Answer "
                  "the prompt (helm.py) or start a seat; it picks up from its own state, not from scratch.")


# --- commands -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def cmd_pass(a: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    """One rumination pass: survey, compare to the last state, speak only on change.

    launchd fires every 300 s and does NOT prevent overlap, so a pass that waits on a slow relay
    could re-read the same `last` state and announce the same line twice. One pass at a time: a
    second pass that arrives mid-pass is skipped, not queued behind it.
    """
    if not a.dry_run:
        lock = STATE_DIR / ".pass.lock"
        STATE_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        holder = open(lock, "a+")
        try:
            fcntl.flock(holder.fileno(), fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        except OSError:
            holder.close()
            log("pass skipped: the previous pass is still running")
            return 0
    else:
        holder = None
    try:
        return _pass(a)
    finally:
        if holder is not None:
            fcntl.flock(holder.fileno(), fcntl.LOCK_UN)
            holder.close()


def _pass(a: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    """One pass: survey, compare to the last state, speak only on change (the lock is in `cmd_pass`).

    Delivery is AT-LEAST-ONCE, on purpose. `state["pending"]` is written after the send, so a crash
    between the send and `save_state` (a window that spans the escalate and hermes sends, potentially
    minutes) leaves the PREVIOUS state on disk and the next pass re-derives and re-sends the same
    lines. Journaling the intent first would turn that into at-most-once — a seat that stays dark
    because a journal says "sent" and nobody sent it — which is the worse failure for the watcher
    whose whole job is a voice. A duplicate is bounded by the pending cap and visible in the log; a
    lost line is invisible.
    """
    now = time.time()
    state = load_state()
    # FIRST, because everything below is code that may be a merge behind (issue #143): a fix merged
    # into djbclark-ade reaches this job only when this checkout moves. It logs its own outcome (and
    # honours the cooldown for a repeated refusal), so the return value is not logged again here. A
    # dry run never moves anyone's checkout.
    if not a.dry_run:
        deploy_update(state, now)
    before = state.get("last", {})
    cur = survey()

    # Resolved BEFORE the items, because the idle-nudge below needs the seat. ONE `--why` probe per
    # pass: whichever of the nudge gate and the quiet branch asks first pays for it, the other reads
    # the cached answer.
    ident, why = owner_session()
    probe: list[tuple[str, str]] = []

    def seat_word() -> str:
        if ident and not probe:
            probe.append(why_blocked(ident))
        return probe[0][0] if probe else ""

    changed = [k for k, v in cur.items() if v != before.get(k)]

    # What is genuinely NEW drives the session line; a re-observation must stay silent.
    before_mail = before.get("mail") or []
    before_job_names = [j.get("name") for j in (before.get("jobs") or [])]
    new_mail = [m for m in cur["mail"] if m not in before_mail]
    new_jobs = [j["name"] for j in cur["jobs"] if j["name"] not in before_job_names]

    # Each item is (key, text). The key is OPT-IN and marks the lines whose condition this pass
    # recomputes from scratch every time, so a queued copy can be checked against it at retry time
    # (see the carry block below). Event items are deliberately KEYLESS: new mail, new jobs and the
    # new-PR announcement are diffs against `state["last"]`, advanced with the pending queue below;
    # after that advance the pass cannot recompute "this was unread", so keying them would silently drop
    # a real announcement the seat never heard. They keep the plain at-least-once retry.
    items: list[tuple[str | None, str]] = []
    if new_mail:
        items.append((None, f"{len(new_mail)} unread peer message(s): {', '.join(new_mail[:3])}"))
    if new_jobs:
        items.append((None, f"{len(new_jobs)} open job(s): {', '.join(new_jobs[:3])}"))
    if cur["stale"]:
        items.append((KEY_STALE, f"stale chain log: {cur['stale']}"))
    if cur["prs"] and not before.get("prs"):
        items.append((None, pr_phrase(cur["prs"])))

    # A job whose `done` file exists but whose record is still open is a real, actionable defect. The
    # key is the LABEL ALONE, not label+names: the finding is "some job is finished but not closed".
    # A queued line naming job A stays true while any job (A or B) is finished-but-open, and a keyed
    # entry is replaced by THIS pass's freshly computed text, so what is delivered names the jobs
    # stuck now, never an A that closed meanwhile. Label+names would drop the entry as "resolved" when
    # A closes even though B is still stuck, trading a refreshed finding for a dropped one; the
    # at-least-once principle says keep the finding and fix the sentence, not the other way round.
    stuck = [j["name"] for j in cur["jobs"] if j["done_exists"]]
    if stuck:
        items.append((KEY_STUCK, f"job(s) finished but not closed: {', '.join(stuck[:3])}"))

    # The one state-like line about WORK instead of about plumbing: the seat is idle and the Tier 1
    # log declares next steps nobody has started. It is recomputed every pass and keyed, so the
    # carry block below retires a queued copy exactly as it does `stale chain log`: no special case.
    # The count is read first (a file) and the probe made only when there is something to say.
    nudge_n = declared_next_steps() if nudge_due(ident, state, now) else 0
    if nudge_n and seat_word() == "idle":
        items.append((KEY_NEXT, f"next move: {nudge_n} declared step(s) still open in the Tier 1 "
                                "log and this seat is idle"))
    lines = [text for _, text in items]

    if a.dry_run:
        print(json.dumps({"changed": changed, "lines": lines, "survey": cur}, indent=1))
        return 10 if lines else 0

    state["last_run"] = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z")
    spoke_session = spoke_djb = False

    # Only the opt-in path probes a candidate that owner_session rejected as non-live. An empty
    # fleet alone is not proof: ask relay's inspect-backed diagnosis, and never spawn on a blind read.
    auto_gone = False
    if AUTO_SEAT:
        candidate = auto_seat_candidate(state)
        tab_note: dict = {}                 # `auto_seat` fills note["tab"] when it could not open one
        target = candidate or ident
        if not target and not _LIVE_ERR:
            target = next((candidate for _, candidate in owner_candidates()), "")
        # A registered successor may have replaced our saved candidate. Prove the resolved owner
        # gone BEFORE falling back to the candidate; never spawn over a different existing owner.
        word = seat_word() if ident else "unknown"
        if candidate and candidate != ident and (not ident or word == "gone"):
            target = candidate
            word = why_blocked(candidate)[0]
            if word in ("idle", "working", "silent") or word.startswith("card:"):
                ident = candidate
                probe[:] = [(word, "auto-seat candidate")]
        elif candidate and candidate != ident and (word in ("idle", "working", "silent")
                                                   or word.startswith("card:")):
            # A live successor is authoritative. Retire the stale candidate so it cannot shadow
            # future registry changes; this is bookkeeping, never a successful-start stamp.
            prefix = f"auto-seat candidate:{ROLE}:"
            state["told"] = {k: v for k, v in state.get("told", {}).items()
                             if not k.startswith(prefix)}
        elif not ident and target:
            word = why_blocked(target)[0]
        # A gone candidate cannot establish that no registered successor exists when fleet reads
        # are broken. Keep live candidate diagnostics above, but refuse spawning while blind.
        if _LIVE_ERR and word == "gone":
            word = "unknown"
        # A seat this pass can SPEAK to but that `auto_seat` did not just confirm was ADOPTED:
        # the listing handed it back, or its start's confirmation was lost. Nothing has tried
        # to give it a tab, so it is as invisible as an unopened fresh one, and a card on it is
        # the case this whole feature exists for (Codex P2 / adversary N4). The test is "the
        # resolved owner IS the candidate", not "the adoption branch ran": once fleet lists the
        # new id, `owner_candidates` yields it and `owner_session` returns it as `ident`, so the
        # branch is skipped - and that fleet-listed case is the COMMON one, which is exactly
        # the hole adversary D1 (2026-10-30) found in the branch-only site.
        if candidate and ident[:8] == candidate[:8] and (
                word in ("idle", "working", "silent") or word.startswith("card:")):
            missing_tab = tab_once(candidate, state, now)
            if missing_tab:
                tab_msg = tab_page(candidate, missing_tab)
                if should_tell_djbclark("auto-seat tab", state, now) and to_djbclark(tab_msg):
                    djb_page_sent("auto-seat tab", state, now)
                    spoke_djb = True
                else:
                    # `should_tell_djbclark`'s docstring: a token page must never die of one
                    # failed send. `tab_once` gives the reason ONCE, so a cooldown-gated or
                    # failed page is CARRIED instead of dropped (adversary D2, 2026-10-30).
                    # One slot, like `undisclosed`: a newer reason replaces an older one.
                    state["undisclosed_tab"] = tab_msg
        started = auto_seat(word, state, now, tab_note)
        auto_gone = word == "gone"
        if auto_gone and (started or should_tell_djbclark("no live session", state, now)):
            said = (f"Dann: no live session for {ROLE!r} (gone). The previous seat stopped answering.")
            if started:
                said += (f" A seat was started automatically at "
                         f"{time.strftime('%H:%M', time.localtime(now))}: {started}.")
                if tab_note.get("tab"):
                    said += f" {tab_note['tab']}"
            if to_djbclark(said):
                djb_page_sent("no live session", state, now)
                spoke_djb = True
            elif tab_note.get("tab"):
                # The gone page retries itself (its cooldown only starts on a send), but the
                # tab reason is not in it next pass - `started` is empty by then - so carry
                # that half alone (adversary D2).
                state["undisclosed_tab"] = tab_page(started or candidate, tab_note["tab"])

    # An unverified send consumes the line on purpose (relay's own text says "do not resend"), so the
    # only thing left is telling him it may have been lost. That admission used to be attempted ONCE,
    # in the branch further down: one failed `hermes send` at that moment consumed the trigger and the
    # line vanished without a word (round 3, F3). It is carried here instead and retried until a send
    # actually returns True — the disclosure only, never the line itself.
    undisclosed = state.get("undisclosed")
    if isinstance(undisclosed, str) and undisclosed:
        if to_djbclark(undisclosed):
            djb_page_sent("unverified send", state, now)
            state.pop("undisclosed", None)
            spoke_djb = True
        log(f"unverified-send disclosure retried: told djbclark={spoke_djb}")

    # The same at-least-once rule for a seat that is alive and has no tab: a page that could
    # not be sent is retried until one returns True, and it waits out the page cooldown rather
    # than jumping it (anti-noise still applies, the reason is only never DROPPED).
    undisclosed_tab = state.get("undisclosed_tab")
    if isinstance(undisclosed_tab, str) and undisclosed_tab:
        if should_tell_djbclark("auto-seat tab", state, now):
            if to_djbclark(undisclosed_tab):
                djb_page_sent("auto-seat tab", state, now)
                state.pop("undisclosed_tab", None)
                spoke_djb = True
            log(f"auto-seat tab page retried: told djbclark={spoke_djb}")

    # The ONE failure a human has to hear about: no live session for this role means the seat is
    # GONE, not busy. Every other line here is for the seat; this one is for him, and it is checked
    # even on a quiet pass — because if the seat died, the survey it reads stops changing too, and
    # the pass then stays quiet forever. That is exactly how the entity went dark for hours at a
    # time with a watcher running the whole while.
    if not ident and not auto_gone and should_tell_djbclark("no live session", state, now):
        if _LIVE_ERR:
            said = (
                f"Dann: the rumination watcher cannot READ the fleet ({_LIVE_ERR}), so it stopped "
                "trying to reach your seat. That is a blind watcher, not necessarily a dead seat — "
                "do not start a new one on this message alone. `python3 -I "
                "~/src/djbclark-ade/skills/session-finder/fleet.py --json` by hand says which."
            )
        else:
            said = (
                f"Dann: no live session for {ROLE!r} ({why}). Nothing is listening; the rumination "
                "watcher has nowhere to speak. A new seat (or the registry row) fixes it."
            )
        if to_djbclark(said):
            # The cooldown starts only now: a failed page must be retried on the next pass, not
            # silenced for six hours by the failure itself.
            djb_page_sent("no live session", state, now)
            spoke_djb = True
        log(f"no live session: {why} (told djbclark={spoke_djb})")

    # A line aimed at a seat that refused it has NOT been delivered. Advance `state["last"]` only
    # alongside its pending queue below, so recovery saves cannot consume an unqueued change. Mail
    # arrives BECAUSE the seat is busy mid-turn, so this is the common collision, not the edge case.
    # Kept at the front, retried next pass, cleared only by a real send.
    #
    # A KEYED entry (see the items above) is a state-like finding the pass recomputes every time, so a
    # queued copy whose key this pass no longer produces describes a condition that has since been
    # fixed: it is dropped, with one log line, instead of being replayed as if it were live (the
    # 067a9868 Tier 1 line was repeated for three passes after the pointer was corrected). One whose
    # condition still holds is replaced by this pass's fresh text and keeps its original `queued_at`.
    # A KEYLESS entry is never dropped here: it keeps the at-least-once retry, verbatim.
    carried = state.get("pending")
    carried = carried if isinstance(carried, list) else []
    unreadable = state.pop("pending_unreadable", 0)
    if unreadable:
        log(f"pending: ignoring {unreadable} unreadable entr{'y' if unreadable == 1 else 'ies'} "
            "in the state file")
    if len(carried) > PENDING_MAX:
        # Eviction is deliberate (a seat busy for a day cannot grow the file), but it LOSES a line:
        # say so in the log, or the last appearance of that line is an unflagged "pending=N".
        log(f"pending: dropping {len(carried) - PENDING_MAX} oldest line(s) over the cap of "
            f"{PENDING_MAX}: {carried[0]['text'][:100]}")
    pending = carried[-PENDING_MAX:]
    fresh = [{"key": k, "text": t, "queued_at": now} for k, t in items]
    fresh_keys = {e["key"]: e for e in fresh if e["key"]}
    fresh_texts = {e["text"]: e for e in fresh}
    front: list[dict] = []
    retry_ages: list[float | None] = []         # one per queued line that is being retried now
    for p in pending:
        if p["key"] and p["key"] not in fresh_keys:
            log(f"pending: dropping resolved {p['key']}")
            continue
        twin = fresh_keys.get(p["key"]) if p["key"] else fresh_texts.get(p["text"])
        if twin is not None:                    # re-derived this pass: keep ONE line, the older age
            if p["queued_at"] is not None:
                twin["queued_at"] = min(twin["queued_at"], p["queued_at"])
        else:
            front.append(p)
        retry_ages.append(None if p["queued_at"] is None else now - p["queued_at"])
    queue = front + fresh
    lines = [e["text"] for e in queue]
    # A quiet pass (nothing survived, nothing new) must also persist the cleared queue.
    state["last"] = cur
    state["pending"] = queue[-PENDING_MAX:]
    known_ages = [x for x in retry_ages if x is not None]
    retry_note = ""
    if retry_ages:
        retry_note = " (retry" + (f", oldest {_age(max(known_ages))}" if known_ages else "") + ")"

    if lines:
        summary = "rumination: " + "; ".join(lines)
        # `to_session` resolves the owner again (registry read + peer_mail resolve, up to 60 s): the
        # pass may have taken a while since the resolution above, and this is the line that has to
        # arrive, so the target is re-checked rather than trusted from a minute ago.
        spoke_session = to_session(summary) if ident else "unreachable"
        # Stamped only now, by a send that SETTLED: a refused or failed one leaves the nudge due, and
        # the keyed pending entry retries it. `unverified` counts: relay says "do not resend", and
        # resending every pass is exactly the duplicate it warns about.
        if any(k == KEY_NEXT for k, _ in items) and (spoke_session in SENT_WORDS
                                                      or spoke_session == "unverified"):
            nudge_sent(state, now)
        # A settled-but-unverified send is NOT a health observation (see UNPROVEN_SEND): the line is
        # done (`SENT_WORDS` settles `pending` below) but the seat has told us nothing, so ask it. The
        # send word stays in the log line; the probe's word is what counts the streak.
        observed = spoke_session
        if ident and spoke_session in UNPROVEN_SEND:
            observed = why_blocked(ident)[0]
            log(f"seat probe after {spoke_session} send: {observed or 'unknown'}")
        dark_item, dark_msg = escalate_dark(observed, ident, state, now)
        if dark_msg:
            if to_djbclark(dark_msg):
                djb_page_sent(dark_item, state, now)
                spoke_djb = True
            log(f"seat {spoke_session}: told djbclark={spoke_djb}")
        # Only items needing HIS decision go to the quiet topic, and at most once per cooldown.
        needs_him = []
        if cur["stale"]:
            needs_him.append("stale chain log")
        if stuck:
            needs_him.append(f"{len(stuck)} job(s) finished but not closed")
        for item in needs_him:
            if should_tell_djbclark(item, state, now) and to_djbclark(f"Dann: {item}"):
                djb_page_sent(item, state, now)
                spoke_djb = True
        if spoke_session in SENT_WORDS:
            state["pending"] = []
        elif spoke_session == "unverified":
            # relay MAY have taken it and its own text says "do not resend". Retrying is how the seat
            # hears the line twice, so the line is dropped ON PURPOSE — and he is told, because an
            # admitted loss beats a duplicate nobody can take back.
            state["pending"] = []
            said = (f"Dann: a rumination line to the {ROLE} seat could not be PROVEN delivered "
                    "(relay.py: it may have arrived). It will not be retried, so it may have been "
                    "lost; the watcher's log has relay.py's own reason.")
            if should_tell_djbclark("unverified send", state, now):
                if to_djbclark(said):
                    djb_page_sent("unverified send", state, now)
                    state.pop("undisclosed", None)
                    spoke_djb = True
                else:
                    # hermes failed on the one attempt: keep the ADMISSION (not the line) to retry.
                    state["undisclosed"] = said
        else:
            # Still owed to the seat: the next pass prepends these and tries again.
            if len(lines) > PENDING_MAX:
                log(f"pending: dropping {len(lines) - PENDING_MAX} oldest line(s) over the cap of "
                    f"{PENDING_MAX}: {lines[0][:100]}")
            state["pending"] = queue[-PENDING_MAX:]
        log(f"spoke session={spoke_session or 'none'} djbclark={spoke_djb}"
            f" pending={len(state['pending'])}{retry_note}: {summary}")
    else:
        # A QUIET pass still has to notice a wedged seat. Without this probe, darkness is only ever
        # seen on a pass that had something to say, and a seat that stops working stops producing
        # changes — so the passes go quiet one after another and the outage reads as "nothing new"
        # all the way through. One `paseo agent inspect` per 5 minutes is the price of noticing.
        # The word here is a `--why` state, so a healthy `idle` must reset the streak (C2): otherwise
        # the streak counts dark passes that were not consecutive and the page says "in a row".
        word = seat_word()
        dark_item, dark_msg = escalate_dark(word, ident, state, now) if word else ("", "")
        if dark_msg and to_djbclark(dark_msg):
            djb_page_sent(dark_item, state, now)
            spoke_djb = True
        log("quiet (nothing new)" + (f"; seat {word}" if word else ""))

    save_state(state)
    if lines:
        print("rumination: " + "; ".join(lines))
        return 10
    if not a.quiet:
        print("quiet: nothing new")
    return 0


def cmd_status(a: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    state = load_state()
    cur = survey()
    stuck = sum(1 for j in cur["jobs"] if j["done_exists"])
    print(f"mail:  {len(cur['mail'])} unread")
    print(f"jobs:  {len(cur['jobs'])} open" + (f" ({stuck} finished-but-open)" if stuck else ""))
    print(f"prs:   {pr_phrase(cur['prs'])}")
    print(f"stale: {cur['stale'] or 'no'}")
    # WHICH COMMIT IS LIVE, not just when it last passed: the job was 5 commits behind master for a
    # day while `last pass` looked perfectly healthy (issue 143).
    rc, branch, _ = _git(["rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD"])
    deploy = state.get("deploy") if isinstance(state.get("deploy"), dict) else {}
    word = deploy.get("word") if isinstance(deploy.get("word"), str) else ""
    print(f"code:  {running_sha()} on {branch if rc == 0 and branch else '?'} in {SELF_REPO}"
          + (f" (last deploy: {word})" if word else ""))
    print(f"last pass: {state.get('last_run') or 'never'}")
    seated = state.get("seated", {})
    latest = max((float(v) for v in seated.values() if 0 <= float(v) <= time.time()), default=None)
    last = time.strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M', time.localtime(latest)) if latest is not None else 'never'
    print("auto-seat: " + (f"on ({len(seated)} role(s) started, last {last})" if AUTO_SEAT else "off"))
    return 0


def cmd_test(a: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    """Prove BOTH channels work: a watcher that cannot be heard is worse than no watcher."""
    ident, src = owner_session()
    outcome = to_session("rumination: channel test from the watcher (no action needed).")
    ok_d = to_djbclark("Dann: quiet-channel test from the rumination watcher (no action needed).")
    if ident:
        print(f"session target: {ident[:8]} (resolved via {src})")
    else:
        print(f"session target: UNRESOLVED - {src}")
    print(f"session relay:  {outcome}")
    print(f"djbclark topic: {'ok' if ok_d else 'FAILED (is inbox-target dann resolvable?)'}")
    return 0 if (outcome in SENT_WORDS or ok_d) else 1


def cmd_install(a: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    """Install the launchd job (macOS). The plist is version-controlled in the repo, not hand-written
    into ~/Library/LaunchAgents, so a fresh machine gets the same job from the same file."""
    if sys.platform != "darwin":
        print("rumination-watch install: launchd is macOS only (exit 69)", file=sys.stderr)
        return 69
    plist = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "launchd" / "com.djbclark.rumination-watch.plist"
    if not plist.is_file():
        print(f"rumination-watch: no plist at {plist}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    dest = HOME / "Library/LaunchAgents" / plist.name
    STATE_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    dest.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    dest.write_text(plist.read_text())
    run(["launchctl", "bootout", f"gui/{os.getuid()}/{plist.stem}"])  # ignore: may not be loaded
    rc, out = run(["launchctl", "bootstrap", f"gui/{os.getuid()}", str(dest)], timeout=30)
    if rc != 0:
        print(f"rumination-watch: bootstrap failed: {out}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    print(f"installed {plist.stem} (every 300 s, background, no model)")
    print(f"state: {STATE_DIR}")
    return 0


def cmd_uninstall(a: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    label = "com.djbclark.rumination-watch"
    run(["launchctl", "bootout", f"gui/{os.getuid()}/{label}"], timeout=30)
    dest = HOME / "Library/LaunchAgents" / f"{label}.plist"
    dest.unlink(missing_ok=True)
    print(f"removed {label} (state kept at {STATE_DIR})")
    return 0


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    # `pass` writes to the checkout it runs from, not only to state, and AGENTS.md rule 8 requires a
    # changed behaviour to appear in the tool's usage text (Codex review on #148). The description is
    # the docstring's first line only, so before this epilog `--help` said nothing about the deploy.
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="rumination-watch",
        description=__doc__.split("\n")[0],
        epilog=(
            "`pass` first fast-forwards THIS checkout to origin/<branch>, so a merged fix reaches the "
            "launchd job without a hand pull: fast-forward only, never a merge commit, reset or stash, "
            "and it refuses rather than forcing on a dirty tracked tree, a detached HEAD, a git "
            "operation in progress, or local commits origin lacks. RW_NO_DEPLOY=1 turns it off, "
            "--dry-run never touches the checkout, and `status` prints the last deploy. "
            "An idle seat whose Tier 1 log declares next steps gets one `next move: N declared "
            "step(s) still open` line per RW_IDLE_NUDGE_S (default 10800 s); RW_IDLE_NUDGE=0 "
            "turns it off. RW_AUTO_SEAT=1 opts into absent-seat starts (OFF by default until "
            "field-tested), authorised by djbclark's 2026-10-30 option-3 ruling. Only a proven "
            "gone diagnosis starts a seat; error/unreadable reads, silent/working/idle seats and "
            "card:* NEVER do. RW_AUTO_SEAT_PROVIDER must be set; no model/thinking flags are passed. "
            "RW_AUTO_SEAT_S (default 3600 s) allows at most one attempt per role per window, confirmed "
            "or not; a start whose id was lost refuses further starts until an authoritative Paseo "
            "listing reconciles it. Nothing retries on the next pass; refusal logs are limited to "
            "one per cooldown. The start runs `env -u PASEO_AGENT_ID` and, once confirmed, opens "
            "the seat's Paseo Desktop tab (`paseo agent open`): a `paseo run` agent has none, a "
            "failed open is logged, paged to djbclark and never fails the start, and one attempt is "
            "also made for a seat adopted by recovery; RW_AUTO_SEAT_NO_OPEN (1/true/yes/on) skips it. "
            "The darkness page still "
            "goes out and names a newly started seat."
        ),
    )
    sub = p.add_subparsers(dest="command", required=True)
    pa = sub.add_parser("pass", help="one rumination pass (the launchd entry point)")
    pa.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true", help="survey and report; send nothing")
    pa.add_argument("--quiet", action="store_true", help="print nothing when there is nothing new")
    pa.set_defaults(func=cmd_pass)
    st = sub.add_parser("status", help="what is open right now")
    st.set_defaults(func=cmd_status)
    te = sub.add_parser("test", help="prove both output channels work")
    te.set_defaults(func=cmd_test)
    ins = sub.add_parser("install", help="install the launchd job (macOS)")
    ins.set_defaults(func=cmd_install)
    un = sub.add_parser("uninstall", help="remove the launchd job (state is kept)")
    un.set_defaults(func=cmd_uninstall)
    args = p.parse_args(argv)
    return args.func(args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
